A||||5||saleenfiero||Is this a tranny problem?||||4||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||05-23-2001||01:38 AM||||I just noticed, once in a while, going around corners at a pretty good clip and quite tight, the auto tranny jumped out of gear or something and let the engine rev freely until I completed the corner.  Do the bands need to be tightened or something?  Flush maybe?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Oreif||05-23-2001||01:57 AM||||This is a typical problem with the 125 trans.<BR>Under hard cornering the torque converter can lose fluid due to the inertia. This means there is not enough pressure on the clutch packs. So it acts like you shifted into neutral. There is a fix for this, You need to buy the "Heavy Duty" kit for the trans made by "Trans-Go". Do a search for shift kits and you'll find the website link and part number. If you are slightly low on trans fluid this will happen more often too.<BR>I have one in mine and it works great.<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">      <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||05-23-2001||02:13 AM||||hmmmm.. I didn't know about this. This is good info... Mine was doing that right after I bought it. Kept getting worse. Turned out to be just an empty tranny! You shoulda smelled the awful burnt smell when I pulled the dipstick out... Luckily with a bit of conditioner and some fluid it is shifting fine now...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-23-2001||10:57 AM||||Any automatic can do this. Happens when you do something that pulls fluid away from the pickup. Pickup then sucks air instead of oil. Hard cornering or breaking can do it. There's not allot you can do about it. A shift kit WILL NOT help this. (It's basically the same problem that happens in the engine oil pan that people are more familiar with.)<P>Mine does this occasionally, but durring hard breaking. Usually means I need fluid.<P>Your first thing should be checking the fluid level. make sure its near the top of the full section. You want it as full as you can get it w/o overfilling. (Transmission hot, idling in park when checking!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-23-2001||11:15 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Any automatic can do this. Happens when you do something that pulls fluid away from the pickup. Pickup then sucks air instead of oil. Hard cornering or breaking can do it. There's not allot you can do about it. A shift kit WILL NOT help this. (It's basically the same problem that happens in the engine oil pan that people are more familiar with.)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ogre, The torque converter does not have an anti-drain back delay valve. The Trans-Go kit supplies this and this is what keeps the pressure up during hard cornering provided the fluid level is correct. Low fluid level and the pick-up sucking air can cause the slipping too.<BR>Prior to installing the shift kit there was one corner that I could get the trans to slip when I went around it. Since I installed the kit, no more slipping.<P><BR>Here's the info for the shift kit:<BR>P/N: SK 125<BR>THM 125 <BR>1980 up <BR>Corrects/prevents/Reduces: <BR>3-2 clunk; lockup shudder; reverse slip; converter drain back delay; <BR>late shifts; soft 1-2; band failure. <P><BR>P/N: 125-S <BR>THM 125 <BR>l980 up <BR>2nd Servo Cushion Spring (OEM replacement) <P>Here's the link of what/how the kit works: <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/transgo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/transgo.htm</A> <P>You can order from them at: <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/order.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/order.htm</A>  <P><BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||80's BOY||05-23-2001||12:43 PM||||i was doing donuts in my 85 auto and when i was done i didn't have 2nd gear. now i need to decide what to do with it. my only guess is that the lack of fluid from the tranny burned up the intermediate plates, thus losing 2nd gear and also no passing gear now too. that's my theory. ||||reg||13
